# App Store credentials — the committed TEMPLATE. Copy it to `native/.env`
# (gitignored, and kept out of the EAS upload by .easignore) and fill it in:
#
#   cp native/.env.example native/.env
#   make store-preflight            # says exactly what is still missing
#
# EVERY VALUE BELOW CARRIES ITS OWN "WHERE DO I GET THIS" — the click path, the
# URL, and the shape to expect. Nothing here can be guessed or derived; each one
# is issued by Apple or by Expo and has to be fetched once.
#
# A LEFTOVER TEMPLATE VALUE COUNTS AS UNSET. `nativeEnv()` in
# scripts/asset-tools/app-store-connect.mjs compares each value against this
# file and treats a match as absent, so a half-filled `.env` reports as missing
# rather than failing later as a bare 401. Do not "fill in" a placeholder by
# leaving it as it is.
#
# WHO READS THIS FILE. fastlane loads it automatically — dotenv searches the
# `fastlane/` folder and its parent, and `bundle exec fastlane metadata` runs
# from `native/`, so this file is the parent. Two repo scripts read it the same
# way, through `nativeEnv()`: `make store-preflight`
# (scripts/store-preflight.mjs) and the Game Center push
# (scripts/game-center-push.mjs). The EAS CLI does NOT read it — `eas build` /
# `eas submit` take their identifiers from `eas.json` (see the bottom of this
# file) and their auth from `eas login` or EXPO_TOKEN.
#
# ONE VALUE PER LINE, and no line breaks inside a value: every reader above
# parses this file line by line, so a wrapped value silently truncates.
#
# NONE of these values belong in the repo: the Apple Account email is personal
# data, the team ids are account-shaped, and the .p8 is a credential that can
# publish on your behalf. `native/*.p8` is gitignored too — keep the key file
# outside the tree anyway if you can.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who fastlane is acting as (fastlane/Appfile reads all three).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Apple Account email the Apple Developer Program is under.
#
# WHERE: it is the address you sign in to https://developer.apple.com/account
# with — the account that holds the paid Apple Developer Program membership
# (99 USD/year; a free Apple Account can build to a device but cannot ship to
# the store). To confirm which one a membership sits on, sign in there: the
# Membership details panel names the account. If several people share the team,
# this is YOUR address, not the Account Holder's — fastlane acts as you.
# SHAPE: an email address.
APPLE_ID=you@example.com

# Apple Developer Team ID — 10 alphanumerics, uppercase.
#
# WHERE: https://developer.apple.com/account → **Membership details** in the
# left sidebar → the "Team ID" row (copy it; the "Entity Name" beside it is the
# legal name, not this). The top-right team switcher in App Store Connect shows
# the same id when an account belongs to more than one team, and Xcode shows it
# in Settings → Accounts beside the team name.
# SHAPE: ABCDE12345 — exactly 10 characters, letters and digits.
APPLE_TEAM_ID=ABCDE12345

# App Store Connect team id — fastlane's `itc_team_id`. A NUMBER, and a
# DIFFERENT thing from the 10-character id above.
#
# USUALLY LEAVE THIS BLANK. It only disambiguates an Apple Account that belongs
# to several App Store Connect teams, and only when authenticating by password:
# the API key below is issued BY one team, so it settles the team on its own.
# `make store-preflight` does not require it.
#
# WHERE: it is NOT anywhere in the App Store Connect UI — Users and Access does
# not show it, which is why hunting for it wastes an afternoon. Get it one of
# three ways:
#   • cd native && bundle exec fastlane spaceship
#     then, at the REPL:  Spaceship::Tunes.select_team
#     (sign in when prompted; it prints every team with its id)
#   • logged in to App Store Connect, open this in the SAME browser and read
#     `contentProviderId` out of the JSON — under
#     data.associatedAccounts[].contentProvider:
#     https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/WebObjects/iTunesConnect.woa/ra/user/detail
#   • ~/.fastlane/spaceship/<apple-id>/ — cached after any fastlane run
# SHAPE: digits only, e.g. 123456789.
# ASC_TEAM_ID=123456789

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# App Store Connect API key. Preferred over an Apple ID password because a .p8
# carries no 2FA session to expire halfway through an upload.
#
# HOW TO ISSUE ONE (all three values below come from this one visit):
#   1. https://appstoreconnect.apple.com → your avatar, top right → **Users and
#      Access** → the **Integrations** tab → **App Store Connect API** in the
#      sidebar.
#   2. Pick **Team Keys**, not Individual Keys. A team key is what a shared
#      pipeline should hold; an individual key is scoped to one person and dies
#      with their access. Issuing a team key needs the **Account Holder** or
#      **Admin** role — if the "+" is greyed out, that is why: ask whoever holds
#      the team for one.
#   3. "+" → name it (the name is a label for you, not part of the key) →
#      **Access: App Manager**. That is the minimum this repo needs — it covers
#      `fastlane metadata`'s listing and screenshot upload AND the Game Center
#      achievement/leaderboard push (`npm run game-center:push`). Developer is
#      not enough for the listing; Admin is more than is needed.
#   4. **Download API Key**. It downloads ONCE and Apple keeps no copy — lose
#      it and the only route is to revoke and issue another. Save it outside
#      this repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The key's own id.
#
# WHERE: the KEY ID column of the row you just generated, and also baked into
# the downloaded filename — `AuthKey_<KEY_ID>.p8`, so reading it off the file
# you saved is the quickest check that the three values match each other.
# SHAPE: 10 alphanumerics, e.g. V4D62Q8UQB.
ASC_KEY_ID=XXXXXXXXXX

# Issuer ID — one per team, shared by every key on it.
#
# WHERE: the same App Store Connect API page, above the key list, next to the
# "Issuer ID" label with a Copy link. It does not change when you rotate a key.
# SHAPE: a lowercase UUID, 8-4-4-4-12.
ASC_ISSUER_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# Where the .p8 lives. Absolute, or relative to `native/` (fastlane runs there).
#
# WHERE: wherever you saved the download in step 4 above. Nothing generates it
# later. `make store-preflight` checks the path actually resolves to a file.
ASC_KEY_PATH=/absolute/path/to/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8
# …or, instead of a path, the key file base64-encoded — for CI, where there is
# no filesystem to put it on. Set ONE of the two, never both: fastlane is handed
# both and silently ignores the loser, and store-preflight fails the pair.
#
# HOW: encode it to a SINGLE line — a wrapped blob breaks every reader of this
# file, and GNU base64 wraps at 76 columns unless told not to.
#   macOS:  base64 -i AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8 | tr -d '\n' | pbcopy
#   Linux:  base64 -w0 AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8
# ASC_KEY_CONTENT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EAS. Only needed to drive builds non-interactively (CI does this with the
# EXPO_TOKEN repo secret); a laptop uses `eas login` instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# An Expo access token. Authenticates the EAS CLI with no interactive login,
# and takes precedence over any `eas login` session on the same machine.
#
# WHERE: https://expo.dev/settings/access-tokens → **Create token** → copy it
# (shown once). Two kinds, and the choice matters:
#   • PERSONAL token — acts as you, across your personal account and every
#     organization you can reach. Fine for a one-off local build.
#   • ROBOT token — https://expo.dev/accounts/<account>/settings/access-tokens,
#     a robot user with a role you pick. It cannot sign in to the dashboard and
#     owns no projects, so its blast radius is bounded. This is the right kind
#     for CI.
# The GitHub workflow that drives EAS (.github/workflows/native.yml, manual
# dispatch only) reads it from the `EXPO_TOKEN` repo secret, so on CI it is set
# there rather than here.
# SHAPE: an opaque string, no fixed prefix. Treat it as a password.
# EXPO_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-time escape hatches. Both are for LOCAL builds only — a store build
# must set neither. Left commented out on purpose. Neither is issued by
# anybody: you choose the value. See docs/configuration.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Drops the iCloud + Game Center entitlements so a build signs on an Apple ID
# that has neither capability enabled. Cloud save, achievements and
# leaderboards all report themselves unavailable. See native/README.md.
# VALUE: the literal `off`; anything else (including unset) keeps them.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_CLOUD_SAVE=off

# Points the WebView at a deployed slot instead of the copy of the game bundled
# inside the app. Debugging only: a store build that streams the website is the
# exact shape App Store guideline 4.2 rejects.
# VALUE: any URL the device can reach — a deploy slot on the live origin, or
# your dev server on the LAN (http://<your-lan-ip>:5173/, not localhost, which
# on a phone means the phone).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GAME_URL=https://game.niclaslindstedt.se/preview/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT here, deliberately: the app's numeric Apple ID (`ascAppId`) and the team
# id EAS submits with (`appleTeamId`). Those go in `eas.json` →
# submit.production.ios, because eas.json is read by the EAS CLI and does not
# expand environment variables. Both are public anyway — so committing them is
# fine — and `make store-preflight` checks they're set.
#
# WHERE ascAppId COMES FROM: App Store Connect → Apps → the app → App Store tab
# → **App Information** in the sidebar → "Apple ID" under General Information.
# Apple assigns it when the app record is first created and it cannot be
# edited, so the record has to exist before there is anything to paste. The
# same number is the `id##########` in the app's App Store URL and the tail of
# its App Store Connect URL (…/apps/1234567890). Digits only.
# WHERE appleTeamId COMES FROM: the same 10-character APPLE_TEAM_ID above —
# store-preflight fails the build if the two disagree.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
